From: yzddmr6 <46088090+yzddmr6@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] jail: allow BTI code mappings in the aarch64 GPU sandbox

On arm64, glibc maps the code of a BTI-enabled shared library with
PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC|PROT_BTI. The GPU device and render server load the
host Mesa driver inside the sandbox, and the inherited aarch64 GPU policy
only allowed PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC, so with a BTI-built Mesa the sandbox
killed the GPU process (SIGSYS on mmap) as soon as a VM with OpenGL
acceleration started. Allow exactly read+execute+BTI for mmap and
mprotect; writable and executable together remains forbidden.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
diff --git a/jail/seccomp/aarch64/gpu_common.policy b/jail/seccomp/aarch64/gpu_common.policy
index b729383..954cafb 100644
--- a/jail/seccomp/aarch64/gpu_common.policy
+++ b/jail/seccomp/aarch64/gpu_common.policy
@@ -79,8 +79,11 @@ prctl: arg0 == PR_SET_NAME || arg0 == PR_GET_NAME || arg0 == PR_SET_VMA || arg0
 ioctl: arg1 & 0x6400 || arg1 & 0x8000 || arg1 == 0x40086200 || arg1 == 0x40087543
 
 ## mmap/mprotect differ from the common_device.policy
-mmap: arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE || arg2 == PROT_NONE || arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC || arg2 == PROT_WRITE || arg2 == PROT_READ
-mprotect: arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE || arg2 == PROT_NONE || arg2 == PROT_READ
+# glibc maps the code of BTI-enabled shared libraries (a distribution or private Mesa, for
+# example) with PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC|PROT_BTI and may apply it again with mprotect. Writable and
+# executable together stays forbidden.
+mmap: arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE || arg2 == PROT_NONE || arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC || arg2 == PROT_WRITE || arg2 == PROT_READ || arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC|PROT_BTI
+mprotect: arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE || arg2 == PROT_NONE || arg2 == PROT_READ || arg2 == PROT_READ|PROT_EXEC|PROT_BTI
 openat: 1
 
 ## Rules specific to pvr
